{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"household-digital-safety-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Household digital safety check-up","description":"Runs a whole-household digital safety check-up in gated steps - inventory, accounts, devices and router, privacy and sharing, scam readiness - and ends with a light routine everyone can keep.","category":"digital-safety","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","maintain"],"role":["parent","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["checklist","plan","conversation"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["security-checkup","family-safety","account-security","home-network","scam-prevention"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["secure-personal-accounts","secure-home-router","set-up-parental-controls","plan-family-scam-safe-word","review-app-permissions"],"personas":["digital-safety-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"household","description":"Who lives in the household with ages and how confident each is with tech, for example \"two adults, a 12-year-old, grandad who lives with us and uses an iPad\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"devices_and_services","description":"Main devices and services, for example \"3 phones, family laptop, smart TV, doorbell camera, provider router, shared Netflix and Amazon\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"worries","description":"Optional. Anything that prompted this, for example \"grandad nearly fell for a scam\", \"son got his first phone\", \"a breach email\".","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"inventory-and-priorities","file":"steps/01-inventory-and-priorities.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"accounts","file":"steps/02-accounts.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"devices-and-router","file":"steps/03-devices-and-router.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"privacy-and-sharing","file":"steps/04-privacy-and-sharing.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"scam-readiness","file":"steps/05-scam-readiness.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/06-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a household through a digital safety check-up one area at a time. After each step the person makes the changes on their own devices and reports back before the next step. Steps go from what protects everything (the main accounts) to what protects people (scam habits).\n\nHousehold: {{household}}\nDevices and services: {{devices_and_services}}\n{{#worries}}Worries: {{worries}}{{/worries}}\n\nThroughout: fit every instruction to the people and devices named, keep each step doable in about an hour, and use generic menu paths that may differ by version. Respect each adult's privacy and autonomy: changes to another adult's devices or accounts happen with them, not for them. Never ask for passwords, codes or card numbers. If anything suggests an account is already taken over, money is being lost, or someone is being tracked or controlled by another person, pause the check-up and deal with that first, with crisis or specialist support where safety is at risk.","steps":[{"id":"inventory-and-priorities","file":"steps/01-inventory-and-priorities.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Inventory and priorities\n\nKnow what you have before changing anything.\n\n1. With the person, list per household member: main email, phone account (Apple or Google), banking and payment apps, social accounts, and devices they use.\n2. List shared things: the router, smart TV and cameras, streaming and shopping accounts, family groups, shared computers.\n3. Mark the highest-risk items: main email accounts (they reset everything else), money accounts, and anyone who is more exposed (a child with a new phone, an older relative targeted by scammers).\n4. Agree the top five fixes for this household, written as a short list.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the inventory and top five before moving to accounts."},{"id":"accounts","file":"steps/02-accounts.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Accounts\n\nProtect the accounts that unlock everything else.\n\n1. For each adult, starting with main email and phone accounts: a password manager (built-in or dedicated), unique passwords on high-risk accounts, two-factor sign-in or passkeys, and backup codes stored offline.\n2. Check recovery email and phone numbers are current and belong to that person.\n3. Review signed-in devices and connected apps; remove what is unknown.\n4. For children, set up accounts under a family group suitable for their age, with the parent as recovery.\n5. Add a carrier account PIN where offered to reduce SIM-swap risk.\n\nStop and ask the person to report which accounts are done before moving to devices."},{"id":"devices-and-router","file":"steps/03-devices-and-router.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Devices and router\n\nMake every device locked, updated and findable.\n\n1. Every phone, tablet and computer: automatic updates on, a screen lock, device finding on, and a backup that works.\n2. Remove apps nobody uses and review sensitive app permissions (location, microphone, camera).\n3. Router: change the default admin password, update firmware, use current Wi-Fi encryption, turn off WPS and remote management if unused, and put smart TVs, cameras and guests on a guest network if the router supports it.\n4. Cameras and smart devices: unique passwords, two-factor on their accounts, remove old shared users.\n5. Note any device that no longer gets updates and plan to replace or limit it.\n\nStop and ask the person to report what was changed before moving to privacy."},{"id":"privacy-and-sharing","file":"steps/04-privacy-and-sharing.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Privacy and sharing\n\nDecide what the household shares and with whom.\n\n1. Social accounts: who can see posts, profile details and tags; children's accounts private by default.\n2. Location sharing: who shares with whom, agreed openly; turn off what nobody needs.\n3. Family groups and purchases: purchase approval for children, and screen time or content limits suited to each child's age, explained to them.\n4. Photos: agree a family rule on posting children's photos and school or location details.\n5. Old accounts and data brokers: note any to close or remove later.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the agreed rules before moving to scam readiness."},{"id":"scam-readiness","file":"steps/05-scam-readiness.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 5: Scam readiness\n\nPrepare people, not just devices.\n\n1. Teach the household rule: pause, and verify through a number or app you already know, before paying, sharing a code or installing anything someone asked for.\n2. Go through the scams most likely for each person (parcel and bank texts, \"Hi Mum\" messages, fake tech support, marketplace and gaming scams for teens) with one real-looking example each.\n3. Agree a family safe word or question for urgent money requests and voice calls.\n4. Write a one-page \"if something goes wrong\" sheet: bank fraud numbers from the cards, how to report scams in their country (examples to check), and who in the family to call.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the sheet is printed or saved before moving to the routine."},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/06-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","text":"# Step 6: Routine\n\nKeep it working with little effort.\n\n1. Monthly (five minutes): check updates have installed and backups ran.\n2. Every three months: review signed-in devices, shared users on cameras and family groups, and location sharing.\n3. Every year: router firmware and passwords, children's limits as they grow, and the \"if something goes wrong\" sheet.\n4. Pick a date and a person responsible for each, and add calendar reminders.\n\nFinish with a short summary of what was done and what is left on the list."}]}
